# kanshi — output configuration, replacing dwl's monrules[] and sway's `output`
# blocks.
#
# river has no output configuration of its own: no modes, no positions, no
# scaling. It implements wlr-output-management and expects an external client to
# drive it, which is kanshi (in Arch galaxy and OpenBSD ports -- one of the
# reasons this stack works on both).
#
# kanshi picks the first profile whose output set matches exactly what is
# connected, and re-applies on hotplug -- so closing the lid and docking are
# handled without a keybind. That is a straight upgrade on dwl, where a monitor
# rule was matched once at createmon() time and a rejected mode was silently
# ignored (which is what segfaulted it).
#
# NOTE: no adaptive sync / VRR here on purpose. dwl asked for it on both outputs
# and a connector refusing it is what took the compositor down. Add
# `adaptive_sync on` per-output once the stack is known good, not before.
#
# NOTE ON 10-BIT: kanshi cannot set colour depth, and neither can river. The
# `render_bit_depth 10` that the sway config had has no equivalent in any wlroots
# compositor -- it was a sway-specific feature. On this OLED that remains a real
# loss versus sway, and it is not something this stack can get back.

# Laptop panel only -- the common case, and the OpenBSD case (no NVIDIA driver
# there, so the internal Intel-driven panel is all there is).
profile laptop {
	output eDP-1 mode 2560x1600@240Hz position 0,0 scale 1
}

# Panel plus the external display, external to the right.
profile docked {
	output eDP-1 mode 2560x1600@240Hz position 0,0 scale 1
	output HDMI-A-1 mode 3840x2160@30Hz position 2560,0 scale 1
}

# External only, i.e. lid shut while docked.
profile external {
	output HDMI-A-1 mode 3840x2160@30Hz position 0,0 scale 1
}

# Fallback: any single unknown output, left enabled at whatever it prefers.
# Without this, plugging in a projector gets you a black screen and no clue why.
profile fallback {
	output eDP-1 enable
}
